      "q": "What is catastrophic backtracking in regular expressions and how do you protect production microservices against it?",
      "solution": "<p><em>A: Catastrophic backtracking occurs when ambiguous, nested, or overlapping quantifiers (such as <code>(a+)+$</code> or <code>(\\w+/?)+$</code>) fail on an input string. CPython's <code>_sre</code> engine uses a backtracking NFA that attempts exponentially many permutations ($O(2^N)$), holding the GIL and freezing worker threads. Defenses include: (1) Bounding input length (e.g. <code>len(input) &lt; 256</code>) prior to matching; (2) Structuring patterns with disjoint, non-overlapping tokens (e.g. <code>\\w+(?:/\\w+)*</code>); (3) Running untrusted patterns in isolated processes with strict OS timeouts; (4) Using linear-time DFA engines like Google's RE2 via <code>google-re2</code>.</em></p>",
